    "question": {
      ":question/id": "abd75f7a-b3fd-5f15-a8e5-599cb5795724",
      ":question/problem": "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{x-y}}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2-9}-2}$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Both radicands must be nonnegative. The complete shifted denominator must also be nonzero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx-y&\\ge 0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad x-y\\ge0\\\\\nx^2+y^2-9&\\ge 0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad x^2+y^2\\ge9\\\\\n\\sqrt{x^2+y^2-9}-2&=0\\\\\n\\sqrt{x^2+y^2-9}&=2\\\\\nx^2+y^2-9&=4\\\\\nx^2+y^2&=13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe right side of the root equation is positive, so squaring is reversible. Exclude this level set. The radicand-zero boundary remains allowed wherever the numerator is real, since the denominator there is $-2$.\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,:\\,\\begin{aligned}x-y &\\ge0\\\\x^2+y^2 &\\ge9\\\\x^2+y^2 &\\ne13\\end{aligned}\\right\\}.\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/requires-calculator": false,
