      ":question/id": "d452c66d-48c5-5656-8d7c-9e6121b7acf9",
      ":question/problem": "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{y^2+x^2-4}+1}$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Both radicands must be nonnegative. The complete shifted denominator must also be nonzero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&\\ge 0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y\\ge0\\\\\ny^2+x^2-4&\\ge 0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y^2+x^2\\ge4\\\\\n\\sqrt{y^2+x^2-4}+1&\\ge 1>0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA principal square root is nonnegative. This denominator is always positive on the real-radicand region, so it has no zero to exclude. Squaring an equation with a negative right side would create extraneous points.\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,:\\,y\\ge0,\\ y^2+x^2\\ge4\\}.\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/requires-calculator": false,
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "3611da4b-c04c-5f79-a495-74b1b894e6fb",
