      ":question/id": "12cdfd79-3d0d-54ec-accd-370f26553442",
      ":question/problem": "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{0-\\left(y\\right)}}{\\sqrt{9-\\left(y^2+x^2\\right)}-1}$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Both radicands must be nonnegative. The complete shifted denominator must also be nonzero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0-\\left(y\\right)&\\ge 0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y\\le0\\\\\n9-\\left(y^2+x^2\\right)&\\ge 0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y^2+x^2\\le9\\\\\n\\sqrt{9-\\left(y^2+x^2\\right)}-1&=0\\\\\n\\sqrt{9-\\left(y^2+x^2\\right)}&=1\\\\\n9-\\left(y^2+x^2\\right)&=1\\\\\ny^2+x^2&=8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe right side of the root equation is positive, so squaring is reversible. Exclude this level set. The radicand-zero boundary remains allowed wherever the numerator is real, since the denominator there is $-1$.\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,:\\,\\begin{aligned}y &\\le0\\\\y^2+x^2 &\\le9\\\\y^2+x^2 &\\ne8\\end{aligned}\\right\\}.\n$$",
